{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A decentralized network is more resilient to correlated failures if and only if it exhibits locally independent but globally non-amplifying failure modes; the evidence that distinguishes truly independent failures from hidden shared causes is whether interventions on a single node's failure rate predictably change the failure rates of geographically or architecturally distant nodes.","counterconditions":["If the decentralized network shares a common critical dependency (e.g., a shared DNS provider, a shared time sync protocol, or a shared cryptographic library) that affects the majority of nodes, then the network is not more resilient.","If the large service has implemented effective isolation boundaries (e.g., bulkheads, cell-based architecture) that prevent internal failure propagation, then the centralized service may be more resilient due to coordinated response capacity.","If the decentralized network lacks the coordination capacity to detect and respond to cascading failures, its theoretical independence may not translate to practical resilience."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Decentralized networks distribute failure modes across independent operational domains. Correlated failures in such networks can only occur through shared external dependencies (e.g., common cloud providers, shared libraries, common upstream supply chains). In contrast, large monolithic services, despite having more internal redundancy, create a single blast radius: internal coupling (shared state, configuration, deployment pipelines, network topologies) means that failures within one subsystem can propagate systemically. The distinguishing evidence is whether localized perturbations (e.g., taking a subset of nodes offline) produce a measurable change in the failure rates of distant, nominally independent nodes. If they do, the failures are not truly independent but share a hidden common cause.","optional_refs":["f8bacb81ddbf78ef60f926a64c0879035edb474a533740791e77b39f69150c9d","63cf10103587bd0c927e03cb5e2043e7518762baea56098f39e79912b7d84a95","2aba9a12cdfd823f00a0152605948b1c86718ad061ce04ea40d74cf20f4acfb4"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.7},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The large service is more resilient because it can afford heterogeneity budgets, in-house root-cause teams, and coordinated rollback mechanisms that decentralized networks cannot match; evidence that failures are truly independent comes from controlled fault injection showing that the failure rate of one component does not increase when another fails, while hidden shared causes are revealed by latent variable analysis on historical incident data.","counterconditions":["If the large service's coordination mechanisms become a single point of failure (e.g., a bad deployment affects all cells simultaneously), then its coordination advantage becomes a liability.","If the decentralized network exhibits sufficient diversity in its technology stacks and operational practices, then the hidden shared cause risk is minimized.","If the large service's heterogeneity budget is insufficient to prevent common-mode failures (e.g., all cells share the same flawed configuration management system), then its redundancy is merely apparent."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Centralized services can implement defense-in-depth strategies that are prohibitively costly for small operators: heterogeneous system designs (preventing common-mode software failures), dedicated reliability engineering teams (rapidly identifying and mitigating correlated risks), and coordinated deployment practices (enabling swift rollback of problematic changes). The apparent independence of failures in decentralized networks often masks shared dependencies (e.g., reliance on common open-source libraries, shared cloud infrastructure) that only become visible during rare correlated events. The evidence to distinguish true independence from hidden correlation requires: (1) controlled fault injection experiments demonstrating that perturbing one node does not increase failure rates in others, and (2) latent variable analysis on historical incident data to identify hidden common causes.","optional_refs":["f8bacb81ddbf78ef60f926a64c0879035edb474a533740791e77b39f69150c9d","63cf10103587bd0c927e03cb5e2043e7518762baea56098f39e79912b7d84a95"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.75},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The resilience comparison is underspecified without bounding the dependency graph; decentralized networks are only more resilient when their internal coupling is strictly lower than the large service's internal coupling, and evidence distinguishing truly independent failures requires constructing a causal DAG (directed acyclic graph) of the system and performing conditional independence tests to determine whether observed failures are marginally independent or only conditionally independent given a latent variable.","counterconditions":["If the dependency graph cannot be fully characterized (e.g., due to unobservable external dependencies), then conditional independence tests may yield misleading results.","If the large service's internal bulkheads are incomplete (e.g., shared state persists across cell boundaries), then its dependency graph is denser than assumed.","If the decentralized network's nodes share more dependencies than the large service's internal subsystems, then the network is not more resilient despite its decentralization."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Resilience to correlated failure is not a function of scale or decentralization per se, but of the structure of the dependency graph. A decentralized network of small services may have high external coupling (shared cloud providers, shared libraries, shared upstream dependencies) that makes it vulnerable to correlated failures, while a large service may implement internal bulkheads that isolate failure domains. The key evidence to distinguish truly independent failures from hidden shared causes involves: (1) constructing a causal DAG of the system's dependencies (both internal and external), (2) collecting failure data across a representative time period, and (3) performing conditional independence tests: if failures are marginally independent, they are truly independent; if they are only conditionally independent given some latent variable, a shared cause exists.","optional_refs":["63cf10103587bd0c927e03cb5e2043e7518762baea56098f39e79912b7d84a95","2aba9a12cdfd823f00a0152605948b1c86718ad061ce04ea40d74cf20f4acfb4"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.55},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Decentralized networks are more resilient when they exhibit genuinely uncorrelated failure modes across diverse operational environments, but the critical evidence to distinguish true independence from hidden shared causes is whether the system's failure correlation structure remains stable under perturbation: if perturbing the operational environment (changing cloud providers, software stacks, geographic regions) changes the correlation structure, then the apparent independence was an artifact of a shared stable environment, not true independence.","counterconditions":["If the environmental perturbation is so extreme that it overwhelms all systems regardless of their independence, then the test is inconclusive.","If the decentralized network cannot practically achieve environmental diversity (e.g., due to cost constraints), then the theoretical resilience is not practically achievable.","If the large service also operates across diverse environments (e.g., multi-region, multi-cloud), then the perturbation test does not discriminate between the two architectures."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"True independence of failures means that the probability of failure in one node is unaffected by the state of other nodes. However, in practice, nodes in a decentralized network may appear independent only because they share a common stable environment. When that environment changes (e.g., a common cloud provider experiences an outage, a shared library has a vulnerability), the failures become correlated. The evidence to distinguish true independence from hidden shared causes is to test the stability of the correlation structure under environmental perturbation: if perturbing the environment changes the correlation structure, the independence was merely apparent; if it remains stable, the independence is more likely genuine.","optional_refs":["63cf10103587bd0c927e03cb5e2043e7518762baea56098f39e79912b7d84a95","2aba9a12cdfd823f00a0152605948b1c86718ad061ce04ea40d74cf20f4acfb4"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.45},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The resilience claim is false: decentralized networks are systematically more vulnerable to correlated failures because they lack the resources to audit their own dependency graphs, and the evidence that distinguishes truly independent failures from hidden shared causes is practically unobtainable without the centralized coordination and measurement infrastructure that only large services can afford.","counterconditions":["If the decentralized network adopts standardized tools and protocols for dependency auditing and incident sharing (e.g., public dependency graphs, shared incident databases), then the measurement disadvantage can be overcome.","If the large service's measurement infrastructure is itself a single point of failure (e.g., the monitoring system fails during an incident), then its measurement advantage is negated.","If the small services are truly minimal in their dependency footprint (e.g., simple static sites with no shared runtime dependencies), then correlated failures are less likely."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Small, independently operated services typically lack the resources to perform comprehensive dependency audits, continuous security monitoring, and coordinated vulnerability management. This means they are more likely to unknowingly share dependencies (e.g., common open-source libraries, common cloud infrastructure) that create correlated failure modes. The evidence to distinguish truly independent failures from hidden shared causes requires measurement infrastructure (e.g., distributed tracing, dependency mapping, cross-organizational incident correlation) that is prohibitively expensive for small operators. Therefore, not only are decentralized networks more vulnerable to correlated failures, but they are also less capable of detecting and responding to them.","optional_refs":["f8bacb81ddbf78ef60f926a64c0879035edb474a533740791e77b39f69150c9d","63cf10103587bd0c927e03cb5e2043e7518762baea56098f39e79912b7d84a95"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.3},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Both the decentralized and centralized architectures are vulnerable to correlated failures through different mechanisms, but the distinguishing evidence is found in the pattern of failure onset: truly independent failures exhibit exponential inter-arrival times and Poisson-distributed failure counts, while hidden shared causes produce clustered failures with heavy-tailed inter-arrival distributions.","counterconditions":["If the failure rate is non-stationary (e.g., due to diurnal traffic patterns), then the Poisson assumption may not hold even for independent failures.","If the hidden shared cause produces failures that are spread out over time (e.g., a slow-burning security vulnerability), then the clustering may not be detectable through inter-arrival time analysis.","If the system has adaptive load shedding that masks the true failure rate, then the observed failure pattern may not reflect the underlying independence structure."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The mathematical structure of failure events reveals whether they are truly independent or share a hidden common cause. If failures are independent and identically distributed, their arrival process follows a Poisson distribution, with exponential inter-arrival times and failure counts that follow a Poisson distribution. If there is a hidden shared cause (e.g., a common dependency that is intermittently failing), failures will cluster: the inter-arrival times will be heavy-tailed (e.g., follow a power-law or Weibull distribution), and the failure counts will be overdispersed relative to a Poisson distribution. This statistical signature is architecture-independent: it applies equally to decentralized and centralized systems. The resilience comparison then depends on which architecture is better able to detect and mitigate the clustered failures that indicate a hidden shared cause.","optional_refs":["63cf10103587bd0c927e03cb5e2043e7518762baea56098f39e79912b7d84a95","2aba9a12cdfd823f00a0152605948b1c86718ad061ce04ea40d74cf20f4acfb4"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.2}],"simulation_proposals":[]}